# orwell — tightly-scoped sudoers drop-in for the web-triggered update (admin "Update" button).
#
# Installed to /etc/sudoers.d/orwell-update (mode 0440) by orwell-install.sh. OPTIONAL: only
# needed when the admin status page's Update button runs the script via the DIRECT detached path
# (ORWELL_UPDATE_SUDO=1). The DEFAULT and preferred door is the root-side flag trigger
# (orwell-ops-update.path / G19b), which needs no sudo at all — the FE just drops a flag file.
#
# Why this exists: the front-end runs as the non-root `orwell` user inside the E85-hardened
# orwell-frontend.service (NoNewPrivileges=yes, empty CapabilityBoundingSet=, ProtectSystem=strict).
# That user cannot git-pull into /opt/orwell, write /etc/systemd, or `systemctl restart`. This grant
# lets it run EXACTLY ONE fixed command — the deploy updater — and nothing else.
#
# Security posture:
#   * A single fully-qualified command, no wildcards, no arguments accepted from the caller — the
#     full argv is fixed here (`/usr/bin/bash /opt/orwell/deploy/orwell-update.sh`). The web tier
#     chooses WHEN, never WHAT or with-which-args.
#   * NOPASSWD (the service user is non-interactive; `sudo -n` never prompts).
#   * NoNewPrivileges=yes in the FE unit normally BLOCKS setuid sudo. Operators using the direct
#     path must add a drop-in clearing it for the FE unit (documented in deploy/README.md); the
#     flag-trigger door avoids this entirely and is recommended.
#
# Adjust the user/paths if APP_DIR is non-default; the installer writes the effective ones.
orwell ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/bash /opt/orwell/deploy/orwell-update.sh
